  "result": "I added `split(cents, parts)` to `shop/money.py` and put its tests in a new `tests/test_money.py`. The whole suite passes: 11 tests, run with `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -t .`. Nothing is committed.\n\n```python\nif parts < 1:\n    raise ValueError(f\"parts must be >= 1: {parts}\")\nbase, extra = divmod(cents, parts)\nreturn [base + 1 if i < extra else base for i in range(parts)]\n```\n\nFor example, `split(10, 3)` returns `[4, 3, 3]`.\n\nThere were no existing money tests, so the new file follows the style of `tests/test_pricing.py`. The tests cover:\n- an even split\n- a split with a remainder, checking the extra cents go to the first elements\n- that the parts always add up to `cents`\n- negative `cents`\n- `ValueError` when `parts` is 0 or -1\n\n**Decision for you:** with negative `cents`, `split(-10, 3)` returns `[-3, -3, -4]`. The total is still right, but the extra cent's worth ends up on the last element rather than the first. If you'd rather get `[-4, -3, -3]`, the fix is to split the absolute value and flip the signs back. Let me know if you want that.",
